## Inspiration and credits

The design-system toolchain (`idesign`, `src/design.rs`, the `design/` format
and the component-preview renderer) is inspired by and adapts ideas from:

- Open Design — https://github.com/nexu-io/open-design (Apache-2.0).
  In particular the layered design-system package (a prose `DESIGN.md` plus a
  machine `tokens.css` contract), a component inventory, and the idea of
  composing a design-aware agent prompt. No Open Design source is copied; the
  Rust model and the on-disk `design/` shape are our own.

Design-system reference material bundled or referenced by Open Design, credited
there and reused here only as format inspiration:

- VoltAgent/awesome-design-md — https://github.com/VoltAgent/awesome-design-md (MIT)
- bergside/awesome-design-skills — https://github.com/bergside/awesome-design-skills
- tw93/kami — https://github.com/tw93/kami (MIT)
- Tom-Opencart/tom-modern-html-style-rule — https://github.com/Tom-Opencart/tom-modern-html-style-rule (MIT)

Design-system packages that reference a brand are aesthetic inspirations, not
official assets of the brands they name.

## On-device naming

`macos/titles.swift` uses Apple's Foundation Models framework, weak-linked so the
app still starts on Macs without it. The macOS component-preview renderer
(`macos/render.swift`) uses system WebKit through a short-lived helper process.
